# "StoryCAD Concepts" (#1626 WBS 5.2), a remake of the 2022 "StoryBuilder Concepts" video,
# from StoryCADTests/AutomationScripts/Concepts-Video-brief.md. One step per brief row.
# Test profile: a regression check of the steps the video shows.
# Video: StoryCADAutomation voice Concepts-Video.scs --out <dir>, then
#        StoryCADAutomation video Concepts-Video.scs --out <dir> --assets <dir> --shared <dir>
# The assets folder (storybuilder-miscellaneous youtube/StoryCAD Concepts Video/assets) holds
# Tangalimlibo.stbx, Terry's outline, used with his permission, and apple-pie.jpg.
# Test run: StoryCADAutomation run Concepts-Video.scs --assets <dir>, with the same assets folder.
script "Concepts Video"
title "StoryCAD Concepts"
launch                                    # the recording starts here; the cards cover the app
expect FileOpenNav exists

step "StoryCAD"
narrate "StoryCAD is a software tool for fiction writers, whose purpose is to assist in creating the outline of a story."
show text "StoryCAD: outlining for fiction writers"

step "An apple pie"
narrate "Imagine you're creating a delicious apple pie. You'll need a recipe and a list of ingredients."
show image "apple-pie.jpg"

step "Story elements"
narrate "The recipe for your story is similar, but instead of apples and sugar and flour, your story's ingredients are story elements, such as characters, the problems they're trying to solve, scenes and settings."
click OpenFromFileNavItem                 # the Open dialog is up from launch
open-file-dialog "{scratch}/outlines/Tangalimlibo.stbx"
expect tree contains "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight"

step "Building blocks"
narrate "Story elements are the building blocks of stories. They're taught in classes and writer's workshops, distilled in a story summary or synopsis, and they're what readers or viewers describe when discussing a story."
open-node tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Problems/Dead (drowned) to the world, Tangalimlibo must decide if she wishes to rejoin her life"
pause 3
open-node tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Characters/Tangalimlibo"
collapse tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Problems"             # 31 rows do not fit; fold what the video has shown
collapse tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Characters"
open-node tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Plot Points/How I was born"

step "Tangible objects"
narrate "Each story element is a tangible object; you can describe it, break it down into smaller parts, and analyze it on its own. Is a character lifelike? Does she have agency? Does a setting contribute to the story's theme, or does it detract? Is your list of scenes linked by cause and effect? Does a scene have drama of its own?"
expand tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Characters"
open-node tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Characters/Tangalimlibo"
tab RoleTab
pause 3
collapse tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Characters"
open-node tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Settings/The river"
tab SettingTab
pause 2
tab SensationsTab
pause 2
open-node tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight/Plot Points/At the celebration a chief's son falls in love with Tangalimlibo"
tab SceneTab

step "Templates"
narrate "In StoryCAD, these story elements are created from templates: forms which organize and assist in thinking about that particular aspect of your story."
click tree "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight"   # the root row has no Invoke; click uses a real pointer
tab StoryIdeaTab
pause 2
tab ConceptTab
pause 2
tab PremiseTab

step "Your story factory"
narrate "Once you understand story elements this way, you can think of them as parts made in your story factory, designing them and assembling them into larger pieces, or modifying them to better fit your design. You can even copy characters and settings from one story to another or into shared story worlds."
menu OpenCreateFileMenuItem
click CreateNewOutlineNavItem
select FileOpenTemplateCombo "Problems and Characters"
set FileOpenProjectNameTextBox "The Night Ferry"
press "Tab"                               # the name binding updates only when focus leaves the box
click PrimaryButton                       # "Create outline"
expect tree contains "The Night Ferry/Characters/Protagonist"

step "Like a file explorer"
narrate "StoryCAD operates somewhat like a File Explorer, but instead of a hierarchy of files and folders, StoryCAD's hierarchy is major story elements, which can be added, deleted, or rearranged. Each major story element (problem, character, setting and scene) contains tabs which provide a progression or workflow to develop it. StoryCAD lets you focus on each story element separately, which simplifies the outlining process."
context-menu tree "The Night Ferry/Characters" "Add Elements/Add Character"
expect tree contains "The Night Ferry/Characters/New Character"
expand tree "The Night Ferry/Problems"
drag tree "The Night Ferry/Characters/New Character" to tree "The Night Ferry/Problems/External Problem"   # under the runner a drop onto a folder does not take; onto a problem it does
expect tree contains "The Night Ferry/Problems/External Problem/New Character"
context-menu tree "The Night Ferry/Problems/External Problem/New Character" "Delete element"
expect-no tree "The Night Ferry/Problems/External Problem/New Character"
open-node tree "The Night Ferry/Characters/Protagonist"
tab RoleTab
pause 1
tab PhysicalTab
pause 1
tab SocialTab

step "Examples"
narrate "StoryCAD also provides thousands of examples for various elements, in the form of pull-down lists of choices, although you're generally not limited to what's in the lists. They're there to encourage your imagination; to eliminate writer's block. Together with StoryCAD's extensive documentation, they provide a short course in fiction writing."
tab RoleTab
expand ArchetypeCombo
pause 3
collapse ArchetypeCombo
tab PhysicalTab
expand BuildCombo
pause 3
collapse BuildCombo

step "Every story idea"
narrate "We believe that every story idea can be developed into a complete outline, ready for you to apply your unique voice and style from first draft to submission."
menu OpenCreateFileMenuItem
click OpenFromFileNavItem
open-file-dialog "{scratch}/outlines/Tangalimlibo.stbx"
expect tree contains "Tangalimlibo, The Walker By Moonlight"

step "Thanks for watching"
narrate "Thanks for watching. Have fun writing!"
pause 3                                   # the app stays on screen while the last line plays
menu ExitMenuItem
expect-exit

closing "Like and subscribe"
